// Buff debug example — simple panic produces Buff-span stack trace (T24).
//
// Run: `buff run examples/debug/panic_demo.buff`
//
// Demonstrates that when a Buff program panics at runtime, the printed
// stack trace references the BUFF source file + line numbers (not the
// generated `.rs` file) when a `.buffmap` source-map sidecar exists
// alongside the compiled binary.
//
// The `helper` function divides by zero — the standard
// "panic_at_runtime" pattern the T24 spec uses for evidence
// collection. With the Buff panic hook installed, the user sees:
//
//   thread 'main' panicked at attempt to divide by zero
//     --> examples/debug/panic_demo.buff:2:13
//
//   Buff stack trace:
//     0: helper (examples/debug/panic_demo.buff:2:13)
//     1: main (examples/debug/panic_demo.buff:5:5)
//
// Without the hook (or with RUST_BACKTRACE=1), the full Rust trace is
// printed AFTER the Buff trace as an escape hatch.

func helper():
    let x = 1 / 0
    return x

func main():
    helper()
